WHAT IS A MONOTONIC STACK?

A stack that always maintains a sorted order
There are only 2 types:

📈 Monotonic Increasing Stack

Elements go from small → big
Example stack:
1, 3, 5, 8

📉 Monotonic Decreasing Stack

Elements go from big → small
Example stack:
10, 7, 4, 2


👉 While inserting new element, we pop elements that break the order
❗ WHY DO WE EVEN NEED IT?
Because many problems ask:
For each element, find nearest element on left/right which is:

greater
smaller

Example patterns:

• Next Greater Element
• Previous Smaller Element
• Stock Span
• Histogram
• Daily Temperatures

🧠 Naive way:

For each element → scan left/right check condition every time 
⏱ O(n²) (slow)

⚡ Monotonic Stack way:

Each element pushed & popped once
⏱ O(n)

🔥 Huge optimization
🎯 EXACT USE (Core Idea)
Monotonic stack helps you quickly answer:
“Who is the next bigger/smaller element for me?”
without checking everything.

📌 SIMPLE EXAMPLE

Array:
[4, 5, 2, 10]


We want Next Greater.
Brute thinking:

For 4 → check 5
For 5 → check 2,10
For 2 → check 10

Too much work 

Stack thinking:
Go from right:
Stack keeps useful future elements only.

Process:

Start:

➡ 10 → stack = [10]

➡ 2 → next greater is 10
stack = [10,2]

➡ 5 → pop 2 (smaller)
next greater is 10
stack = [10,5]

➡ 4 → next greater is 5

END

